WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.344
What a wait is made of.

2
00:00:01.624 --> 00:00:04.099
A latency complaint is not one number.

3
00:00:04.379 --> 00:00:12.571
Four measurements name where a wait comes from: Time to first token (TTFT): from sending the request until the response starts.

4
00:00:12.851 --> 00:00:17.245
It covers the network, queueing, and reading whatever part of the prompt isn’t cached.

5
00:00:17.525 --> 00:00:23.541
Time to first visible output: until the first text or tool call a person or program can use.

6
00:00:23.821 --> 00:00:30.029
When the model thinks first, this comes only after the thinking, so it can be many seconds after TTFT.

7
00:00:30.309 --> 00:00:36.005
Many people say “TTFT” when they mean this one, so check which your logs actually measure.

8
00:00:36.285 --> 00:00:39.208
Time to finish: until the response is complete.

9
00:00:39.488 --> 00:00:45.589
For an agent, the wait that matters is the whole task: every call in the loop plus everything between them.

10
00:00:45.869 --> 00:00:51.587
Output speed (OTPS): tokens per second between the first token and the last, thinking included.

11
00:00:51.867 --> 00:01:05.776
Anthropic’s latency cookbook ties these together with a first-order equation for a task: task time is about calls times TTFT, plus output tokens divided by output speed, plus everything outside the model.

12
00:01:06.056 --> 00:01:13.160
“Everything outside” means tool execution, network hops, queues, retries after rate limits, and your own processing.

13
00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:24.768
The equation gives you five ways to shorten a task: fewer calls, shorter TTFT, fewer output tokens (thinking included), faster output, and less time outside the model.

14
00:01:25.048 --> 00:01:29.891
It sets the order in which to look; only measurement says which lever wins on your workload.

15
00:01:30.171 --> 00:01:39.451
Anthropic’s own reducing-latency page adds a warning worth taping to your monitor: engineer a prompt that works well first, then reduce latency.

16
00:01:39.731 --> 00:01:44.424
Optimizing too early can stop you from discovering what top performance looks like.
